00:02:23.789 --> 00:02:25.949
seen come with the Blueprint.

34
00:02:25.949 --> 00:02:30.469
That's given to all actors, you can actually get rid of it if you want, but the main

35
00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:34.889
thing to keep in mind is that you can child and parent the components like we did with

36
00:02:34.889 --> 00:02:41.240
the door and the door frame, and all components will exist in that list.

37
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:45.139
We have level Blueprints, which we didn't touch upon in this example today because

38
00:02:45.139 --> 00:02:50.240
we put all the functionality within that Blueprint, but if we wanted to add any functionality

39
00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.919
to the level Blueprint, we could go ahead and do that.

40
00:02:53.919 --> 00:02:59.439
You can go to the level Blueprint by going into your first person map, and you can

41
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:05.699
select the Blueprint graph here, and open level Blueprint, and there's a level Blueprint

42
00:03:05.699 --> 00:03:12.099
in here where if we type begin play, and again you don't need to do this, and if

43
00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:19.599
I print the string here, and I say level loaded, we'll just give it one exclamation

44
00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:20.599
mark now.

45
00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:25.620
And if we compile and save, and when we play the level, you can see that it says

46
00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.159
level loaded in the top left hand corner of the screen there.

47
00:03:29.159 --> 00:03:33.780
And all that means is that we've got some information specific to the context of this

48
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.819
level that we can do here.

49
00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:39.060
Now you only really want to be doing certain things on level Blueprint, because say you

50
00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.699
go from one level to the next, if you put all your door code on this level Blueprint,

51
00:03:43.699 --> 00:03:48.699
if you went to a new level, say level 2, you'd have to redo all that door functionality.

52
00:03:48.699 --> 00:03:53.919
So the good thing about us housing it inside of a door Blueprint like this one, is that you can

53
00:03:53.919 --> 00:03:59.099
use this door across multiple levels, and all the information is housed within that

54
00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:00.099
Blueprint.

55
00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:03.659
So that's really useful to know.

56
00:04:03.659 --> 00:04:08.919
You can have data only Blueprints, which only contain code and script, and more often

57
00:04:08.919 --> 00:04:12.120
than not this will be the programmers on your team that are using them, but you

58
00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:17.180
can use them to tweak properties or set items with variations, and they're impacted

59
00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:20.079
in more of a compact editor.

60
00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:24.980
So again, just good information, useful information for you to know about there.

61
00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:30.639
We didn't touch upon Blueprint interfaces, as I said at the start of the tutorial here,

62
00:04:30.639 --> 00:04:35.199
Blueprint interfaces can be used as a communication method from one Blueprint to another, so

63
00:04:35.199 --> 00:04:40.939
as you continue your scripting journey, definitely look out for Blueprint interfaces, because

64
00:04:40.939 --> 00:04:49.170
it can be a really powerful way of just sending a message from one Blueprint to another.

65
00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:54.490
Blueprint communications, we didn't touch upon casting, but you can cast, you can get

66
00:04:54.529 --> 00:04:58.810
an object reference and cast to say, is this the thing that you're looking for?

67
00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:05.050
Again, Blueprint interfaces are something that where you can send a message from one Blueprint

68
00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:10.810
to another, and you can also use event dispatches, which binds one or multiple events,

69
00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:15.410
and those can actually be called on the level Blueprint, a little bit outside of the

70
00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:22.600
scope of today's tutorial, but just to make you aware of these interface and functions.

71
00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:26.879
And the way we think about Blueprint scripting is that it's a compiler software that turns

72
00:05:26.879 --> 00:05:33.519
source code into machine language that can be executed by the CPU, so that requires changing

73
00:05:33.519 --> 00:05:37.959
depending on the hardware and operating system, and when the script has been created,

74
00:05:37.959 --> 00:05:38.959
it needs to be compiled.

75
00:05:38.959 --> 00:05:43.199
So as you saw in our example, we had to keep compiling and saving, and that just makes

76
00:05:43.199 --> 00:05:49.279
sure Unreal knows what new code has been added so that it can execute it correctly.

77
00:05:49.279 --> 00:05:53.519
And then to finish off, we just want to talk about the Blueprint scripting framework here,

78
00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:57.600
and it's essentially Blueprints are all compiled into bytecode, so it's processed

79
00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:03.180
by a virtual machine, the virtual machine translates it by bytecode into the instructions,

80
00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:07.120
and the hardware can understand what is actually being processed, and then we nativize

81
00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:13.319
the Blueprint so we can take the Blueprint visual language and convert it to C++, which

82
00:06:13.319 --> 00:06:17.160
essentially just means, you know, you can have larger projects, it processes the code

83
00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:22.240
more quickly, and that's the kind of overview of Blueprint scripting framework.
